# 7. Single Row Functions — Final Revision

```text
SINGLE ROW FUNCTIONS
│
├── CHARACTER FUNCTIONS
│   ├── UPPER()
│   ├── LOWER()
│   ├── INITCAP()
│   ├── CONCAT()
│   ├── LENGTH()
│   ├── SUBSTR()
│   ├── INSTR()
│   ├── TRIM()
│   ├── LPAD()
│   └── RPAD()
│
├── NUMBER FUNCTIONS
│   ├── ROUND()
│   ├── TRUNC()
│   └── MOD()
│
├── GENERAL FUNCTIONS
│   ├── NVL()
│   ├── NVL2()
│   └── CASE
│
├── DATE FUNCTIONS
│   ├── SYSDATE
│   ├── MONTHS_BETWEEN()
│   ├── ADD_MONTHS()
│   ├── NEXT_DAY()
│   └── LAST_DAY()
│
└── CONVERSION FUNCTIONS
    └── TO_CHAR()
```

### Most important difference

```text
Single Row Function
        ↓
One result for EACH row

Multiple Row Function
        ↓
One result for a GROUP
```

For example:

```sql
SELECT UPPER(f_name)
FROM emp;
```

gives one result for every employee.

Whereas:

```sql
SELECT SUM(salary)
FROM emp;
```

gives one overall result.

